  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 333 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nSouthwest flow aloft begins to strengthen Monday ahead of a closed \nupper trough that will digging southeastward across California \ntoward the Desert Southwest. Leeward troughing will deepen through \nthe day across the western High Plains, with a moist southeast low-\nlevel flow in place to the east of the troughing across the area. \nSurface dew points around 60F should be in place, which is rather \nimpressive for the end of September. PWATS will be on the increase \nonce again due to a combination of low-level Gulf moisture, and mid \nand upper-level sub-tropical moisture being pulled northeastward \nfrom Mexico and the Desert Southwest. ECMWF ENS average mean PWATS \nwill be around 1.1 inches Monday afternoon. As a weak shortwave \nadvances across the area Monday afternoon into Monday night, \nconvection is likely to develop near the leeward trough Monday \nafternoon across western Nebraska within the moist up-slope flow \nregime. Aided by the weak shortwave and a modest southerly low-level \njet, convection should grow upscale as it moves eastward across the \narea Monday night. Locally heavy rainfall rates could become a \nconcern due to the anomalously high amount of moisture that will be \nin place. ECMWF ENS 24 hour mean QPF totals ending at 7am Tuesday \nare in the 0.2 to 0.4 tenth of an inch range. The heaviest mean \ntotals around 0.4\" reside across southwest into central Nebraska. \nThere will likely be localized heavier amounts greater than 1\" in \nspots.  \n  \nAs we move toward the middle of the week the weather remains \nunsettled. The Desert Southwest closed upper trough will swing \neastward toward the Southern Plains. At the same time, the remnants \nof Hurricane Polo and it's associated tropical moisture, will be \npulled northeast and become absorbed into the upper circulation of \nthe upper low. There continues to be low confidence on how this \ncould affect us. The consensus seems to be that most of the rainfall \n(potentially heavy) will focus south and southeast of our area \nWednesday and Thursday. This is largely due to strong northern \nstream energy (westerly flow aloft) across the Northern Plains. This \nessentially helps steer upper low tracking in a more easterly \ndirection across the Southern Plains. Will continue to monitor.",
